# Introduction

Mortgage lending is built on uncertainty. Every approved home loan represents a judgement about the future: whether a borrower will continue to meet repayment obligations despite changes in income, interest rates, employment, or wider economic conditions. When those judgements fail at scale, the consequences can extend far beyond individual households. The 2008 global financial crisis demonstrated how rising mortgage defaults can destabilize financial institutions, weaken housing markets, and create widespread economic disruption. As a result, accurately identifying high-risk borrowers remains one of the most important challenges in modern credit risk management.

At the same time, financial institutions now have access to increasingly large volumes of borrower and loan data, creating new opportunities for predictive analytics and machine learning to support lending decisions. However, improving predictive performance alone is not enough. Lenders must also understand which borrower characteristics are associated with elevated default risk and whether complex machine learning models provide meaningful advantages over simpler, more interpretable approaches.

This analysis examines more than 420,000 residential mortgage loans acquired by Fannie Mae in the United States to investigate the factors most strongly linked to mortgage borrower default. Using borrower financial characteristics, loan structure information, and repayment outcomes, the study compares logistic regression, random forest, and XGBoost models while exploring the broader drivers of mortgage risk.

The findings show that weaker credit quality, higher debt burdens, and greater loan leverage were consistently associated with increased default risk. Interestingly, simpler interpretable models performed competitively against more advanced machine learning techniques, reinforcing the continuing importance of traditional financial risk indicators in lending and risk assessment decisions.

# The Analysis

The analysis used a large-scale dataset containing more than 420,000 residential mortgage loans acquired by Fannie Mae in the United States. Each loan included information about borrower finances, loan structure, property characteristics, and repayment outcomes, making it possible to explore how different financial and lending factors relate to mortgage default risk in real-world conditions.

Rather than examining every available variable, the analysis focused on borrower and loan characteristics that lenders would realistically consider during the loan approval process. Particular attention was given to factors such as borrower credit quality, debt burden, loan leverage, interest rates, occupancy status, and mortgage insurance. Variables directly linked to events occurring after serious delinquency or foreclosure were intentionally excluded to ensure that the models reflected realistic prediction conditions rather than hindsight information.

The investigation began with exploratory analysis to better understand the broader patterns within the dataset. One of the clearest early findings was that mortgage defaults were relatively rare, representing only a small proportion of all loans.

```{r}
#| label: setup
#| include: false

library(tidyverse)
library(tidymodels)
library(vip)
library(janitor)
library(skimr)

set.seed(123)
```

```{r}
#| label: load-data
#| include: false
fannie_mae <- read_csv("fannie_mae.csv", show_col_types = FALSE)

glimpse(fannie_mae)
dim(fannie_mae)
```

```{r}
#| label: create-target
#| include: false
fannie_mae <- fannie_mae %>%
  mutate(
    default = if_else(!is.na(F120_DTE), "Default", "No Default"),
    default = factor(default, levels = c("Default", "No Default"))
  )

fannie_mae %>%
  count(default) %>%
  mutate(percent = round(n / sum(n) * 100, 2))
```

```{r}
#| label: remove-leakage
#| include: false
remove_vars <- c(
  "F30_DTE", "F60_DTE", "F90_DTE", "F120_DTE",
  "F30_UPB", "F60_UPB", "F90_UPB", "F120_UPB",
  "NET_LOSS", "NET_SEV",
  "LAST_STAT",
  "FCC_COST", "PP_COST", "AR_COST",
  "IE_COST", "TAX_COST",
  "NS_PROCS", "CE_PROCS",
  "RMW_PROCS", "O_PROCS",
  "MOD_FLAG", "FMOD_DTE",
  "MODTOT_COST"
)

fannie_mae <- fannie_mae %>%
  select(-any_of(remove_vars))
```

```{r}
#| label: select-predictors
#| include: false
selected_vars <- c(
  "default",
  "CSCORE_MN",
  "dti",
  "oltv",
  "ocltv",
  "orig_rt",
  "orig_amt",
  "FTHB_FLG",
  "purpose",
  "PROP_TYP",
  "NUM_UNIT",
  "occ_stat",
  "num_bo",
  "MI_TYPE",
  "mi_pct",
  "state"
