expand_limits(y = 105) +
  theme_minimal() +
  theme(
    legend.position = "none",
    plot.title = element_text(face = "bold")
  )
```

Mortgage defaults accounted for just over 3% of all loans within the dataset, highlighting the difficulty lenders face when identifying high-risk borrowers within a predominantly low-risk portfolio. This imbalance also reinforced the importance of using evaluation measures beyond simple accuracy when assessing predictive models.

Another clear pattern emerged when examining borrower credit quality. Default rates increased substantially among borrowers with weaker credit histories, suggesting that credit score remained one of the strongest indicators of mortgage repayment behaviour.

```{r}
#| label: credit-score-groups
#| include: false
fannie_mae_model <- fannie_mae_model %>%
  mutate(
    credit_group = case_when(
      CSCORE_MN < 620 ~ "Poor",
      CSCORE_MN < 680 ~ "Fair",
      CSCORE_MN < 740 ~ "Good",
      TRUE ~ "Excellent"
    ),
    credit_group = factor(
      credit_group,
      levels = c("Poor", "Fair", "Good", "Excellent")
    )
  )
```

```{r}
#| label: credit-score-default-rate
#| echo: false
#| warning: false
#| message: false
fannie_mae_model %>%
  group_by(credit_group, default) %>%
  summarise(n = n(), .groups = "drop") %>%
  group_by(credit_group) %>%
  mutate(prop = n / sum(n)) %>%
  filter(default == "Default") %>%
  ggplot(aes(x = credit_group, y = prop, fill = credit_group)) +
  geom_col() +
  geom_text(
    aes(label = scales::percent(prop, accuracy = 0.1)),
    vjust = -0.3,
    size = 5
  ) +
  scale_y_continuous(labels = scales::percent) +
  labs(
    title = "Lower Credit Scores Were Associated with Higher Default Risk",
    x = "Credit Score Category",
    y = "Default Rate"
  ) +
  expand_limits(y = 0.12) +
  theme_minimal() +
  theme(
    legend.position = "none",
    plot.title = element_text(face = "bold")
  )
```

Borrowers classified within lower credit score categories experienced significantly higher default rates than borrowers with stronger credit profiles. This finding reinforced the importance of borrower creditworthiness in long-term mortgage performance and aligned closely with traditional credit risk assessment practices used within the lending industry.

The analysis also revealed a strong relationship between debt burden and mortgage risk. As borrower debt-to-income ratios increased, default rates rose steadily across the dataset.

