Borrowers carrying higher levels of debt relative to income were substantially more likely to experience mortgage delinquency. This suggests that financial stress and repayment pressure remain major contributors to mortgage default risk, even among borrowers who initially qualified for loan approval.

Loan leverage produced a similarly important pattern. Borrowers with higher loan-to-value ratios consistently displayed elevated default rates compared with borrowers who held greater equity in their homes.

