Higher loan-to-value ratios were associated with noticeably greater default risk, indicating that borrowers with limited equity exposure were more financially vulnerable when repayment conditions became more difficult. Together, these early findings established a consistent pattern linking mortgage default risk to borrower financial resilience, debt burden, and loan leverage.

To further investigate these relationships, three predictive modelling approaches were explored: logistic regression, random forest, and XGBoost. Logistic regression was included as a transparent baseline model that prioritized interpretability, while the machine learning models were used to determine whether more flexible approaches could better capture complex borrower risk patterns within the data. Model performance was evaluated primarily using ROC AUC, which provided a more reliable measure of predictive quality for the highly imbalanced mortgage default classification problem. ROC AUC was particularly important because mortgage defaults were relatively rare within the dataset, making simple accuracy less informative for assessing whether models could successfully identify genuinely high-risk borrowers.

# Key Findings

```{r}
#| label: train-test-split
#| include: false
set.seed(123)

split <- initial_split(
  fannie_mae_model,
  prop = 0.8,
  strata = default
)

train_data <- training(split)
test_data  <- testing(split)

#| label: final-model-data

train_data <- train_data %>%
  select(-credit_group, -dti_group, -ltv_group)

test_data <- test_data %>%
  select(-credit_group, -dti_group, -ltv_group)


dim(train_data)
dim(test_data)

```

```{r}
#| label: recipe
#| include: false
credit_recipe <- recipe(default ~ ., data = train_data) %>%
  
  # Impute numeric missing values
  step_impute_median(all_numeric_predictors()) %>%
  
  # Impute categorical missing values
  step_impute_mode(all_nominal_predictors()) %>%
  
  # Convert categorical variables into dummy variables
  step_dummy(all_nominal_predictors()) %>%
  
  # Remove zero variance predictors
  step_zv(all_predictors()) %>%
  
  # Normalize numeric predictors
  step_normalize(all_numeric_predictors())

prep(credit_recipe)
```

```{r}
#| label: logistic-model
#| include: false
log_reg_model <- logistic_reg() %>%
  set_engine("glm")

#| label: logistic-workflow

log_reg_workflow <- workflow() %>%
  add_recipe(credit_recipe) %>%
  add_model(log_reg_model)

#| label: logistic-fit

log_reg_fit <- fit(
  log_reg_workflow,
  data = train_data
)
```

```{r}
#| label: logistic-predictions
#| include: false
log_reg_predictions <- predict(
  log_reg_fit,
  test_data,
  type = "prob"
) %>%
  bind_cols(
    predict(log_reg_fit, test_data),
    test_data %>% select(default)
  )
```

```{r}
#| label: logistic-roc
#| include: false
roc_auc(
  log_reg_predictions,
  truth = default,
  .pred_Default
)
```

```{r}
#| label: random-forest-model
#| include: false
rf_model <- rand_forest(
  trees = 100,
  mtry = 4,
  min_n = 20
) %>%
  set_engine("ranger") %>%
  set_mode("classification")

#| label: random-forest-workflow
#| include: false
rf_workflow <- workflow() %>%
  add_recipe(credit_recipe) %>%
  add_model(rf_model)
```

```{r}
#| label: random-forest-fit
#| include: false
rf_fit <- fit(
  rf_workflow,
  data = train_data
)
```

```{r}
#| label: random-forest-predictions
#| include: false
rf_predictions <- predict(
  rf_fit,
  test_data,
  type = "prob"
) %>%
  bind_cols(
    predict(rf_fit, test_data),
    test_data %>% select(default)
  )
```

```{r}
#| label: random-forest-roc
#| include: false
roc_auc(
  rf_predictions,
  truth = default,
  .pred_Default
)
```

```{r}
#| include: false
library(xgboost)
```

```{r}
#| label: xgboost-model
#| include: false
