The predictive modelling results showed that all three approaches demonstrated a reasonable ability to distinguish higher-risk borrowers from lower-risk borrowers. However, the differences between models were smaller than expected, suggesting that many of the key drivers of mortgage default were already captured effectively through traditional financial risk indicators.

```{r}
#| label: model-comparison
#| echo: false
#| warning: false
#| message: false
model_results <- tibble(
  Model = c(
    "Logistic Regression",
    "Random Forest",
    "XGBoost"
  ),
  ROC_AUC = c(
    0.757,
    0.746,
    0.750
  )
)
model_results %>%
  mutate(ROC_AUC = round(ROC_AUC, 3)) %>%
  knitr::kable(caption = "Model performance comparison")
```

Although the machine learning models provided slightly different classification behaviour, logistic regression ultimately produced the strongest overall predictive performance, achieving an ROC AUC of approximately 0.757. Random forest and XGBoost models performed similarly but did not substantially outperform the simpler baseline approach.

