```

One of the most interesting findings from the analysis was that more advanced machine learning approaches did not meaningfully improve predictive performance over the simpler logistic regression model, despite their greater flexibility and complexity. This suggests that mortgage default behaviour within the dataset was driven largely by relatively stable and interpretable financial relationships rather than highly complex nonlinear interactions.

From a practical perspective, this finding is important for lenders and financial institutions. Simpler models such as logistic regression are easier to interpret, audit, explain to regulators, and deploy operationally. In highly regulated financial environments, transparency can be just as valuable as marginal improvements in predictive accuracy.

To further understand the factors influencing mortgage risk, the analysis examined the most influential predictors identified by the logistic regression model.

```{r}
#| label: logistic-coefficients
#| echo: false
#| warning: false
#| message: false
log_reg_fit %>%
  extract_fit_parsnip() %>%
  tidy() %>%
  filter(term != "(Intercept)") %>%
  arrange(desc(abs(estimate))) %>%
  select(term, estimate) %>%
  mutate(estimate = round(estimate, 3)) %>%
  slice(1:10) %>%
  knitr::kable(caption = "Most influential predictors from the logistic regression model")
```

The coefficient analysis identified credit score, combined loan-to-value ratio, debt-to-income ratio, number of borrowers, and loan amount as highly influential predictors. Together with the exploratory plots, these results reinforce that borrower credit quality, financial stress, and loan leverage are central to default risk.

Several additional patterns also emerged. Borrowers with multiple co-borrowers displayed different repayment behaviour compared with single-borrower loans, while mortgage insurance and loan purpose variables also contributed meaningfully to default prediction. Together, these findings suggest that mortgage default risk is shaped not by a single factor, but by the interaction between borrower financial resilience, repayment pressure, and loan structure.

The ROC curve comparison further illustrated the relatively similar performance of the three modelling approaches while confirming the competitive performance of logistic regression across different classification thresholds.

```{r}
#| label: roc-data
#| include: false
log_reg_roc <- roc_curve(
  log_reg_predictions,
  truth = default,
  .pred_Default
) %>%
  mutate(Model = "Logistic Regression")

rf_roc <- roc_curve(
  rf_predictions,
  truth = default,
  .pred_Default
) %>%
  mutate(Model = "Random Forest")

xgb_roc <- roc_curve(
  xgb_predictions,
  truth = default,
  .pred_Default
) %>%
  mutate(Model = "XGBoost")

roc_data <- bind_rows(
  log_reg_roc,
  rf_roc,
  xgb_roc
)
```

```{r}
#| label: roc-curve-plot
#| echo: false
#| warning: false
#| message: false
ggplot(roc_data,
