  )
```

Overall, the findings demonstrated that traditional financial risk indicators remain highly informative in mortgage lending environments. While advanced machine learning models may provide incremental predictive improvements in some situations, this analysis suggests that transparent and interpretable approaches can still perform competitively when supported by strong borrower and loan-level financial information.

# Implications

The findings from this analysis highlight how strongly mortgage default risk is linked to borrower financial resilience. Borrowers with weaker credit histories, higher debt burdens, and limited equity exposure consistently displayed greater repayment risk across the dataset. For lenders, this reinforces the importance of evaluating affordability and financial stress holistically rather than relying on single approval thresholds alone.

These insights could support more targeted lending decisions and earlier risk intervention strategies. Borrowers showing combinations of high debt-to-income ratios and high loan-to-value ratios may benefit from closer monitoring or additional financial support before severe delinquency occurs. From a portfolio management perspective, identifying these higher-risk patterns early may also help institutions manage exposure more effectively during periods of economic uncertainty or rising interest rates.

One of the more interesting findings was that logistic regression performed competitively against more advanced machine learning approaches. In practice, this matters because simpler models are often easier to explain, audit, and justify within highly regulated lending environments. While complex machine learning models may offer incremental predictive improvements, financial institutions must balance predictive performance against transparency, fairness, and regulatory accountability.

The analysis also highlights the broader importance of responsible use of predictive analytics within mortgage lending. Predictive models can improve risk assessment efficiency, but they should support, not replace human judgement. As lending decisions increasingly rely on data-driven systems, ensuring fairness, transparency, and ongoing monitoring will remain essential for both financial institutions and borrowers.

# Conclusion

This analysis explored the factors most strongly associated with mortgage borrower default using more than 420,000 residential mortgage loans acquired by Fannie Mae in the United States. Across both the exploratory analysis and predictive modelling stages, the findings consistently showed that weaker credit quality, higher debt burdens, and greater loan leverage were strongly linked to elevated default risk.

While advanced machine learning approaches such as random forest and XGBoost performed reasonably well, the results showed that simpler interpretable models remained highly competitive. This suggests that many of the key drivers of mortgage default are still grounded in relatively stable financial relationships that lenders have relied on for decades. In practice, this is important because transparent models are often easier to explain, regulate, and apply within real lending environments.

More broadly, the findings reinforce the importance of balancing predictive accuracy with responsible and explainable decision-making. As financial institutions continue to adopt data-driven risk assessment systems, predictive models should support human judgement rather than replace it entirely.

Although the analysis identified several strong predictors of mortgage default, the data set could not fully capture external influences such as changing economic conditions, employment shocks, or borrower behaviour over time. Future work could explore how broader economic indicators and time-based trends influence mortgage risk under different market conditions.

Overall, the analysis demonstrates that effective mortgage risk assessment depends not only on predictive performance, but also on understanding the financial realities that shape borrower vulnerability and long-term repayment behaviour.
